<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pandulf Ironhead</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 981</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Pandulf I of Capua</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pandulf Ironhead was prince of Capua and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a> from 961 and a loyal ally of the Ottonian emperors in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. With imperial backing he also gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spoleto">Spoleto</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camerino">Camerino</a> and, in 978, Salerno, briefly uniting most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> southern Italy. His lands were divided among his sons after his death in 981.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pandulf-ironhead.jpg" alt="Pandulf Ironhead" width="320" height="393" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Torre_di_capodiferro_Cartolina.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pandulf was the son of Landulf II and Yvantia, and from 943, when his grandfather Landulf I died, he ruled jointly with his father, and from 959 also with his brother Landulf III. About 955 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-xii">Pope John XII</a> led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, Tuscans and Spoletans against Landulf II and Pandulf, but Gisulf I of Salerno came to their aid and the pope made peace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terracina">Terracina</a>, beginning a lasting alliance between Gisulf and Pandulf. After their father's death the brothers were in principle joint rulers of one indivisible principality, but in practice Pandulf held Capua and Landulf Benevento.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When a revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> drove out Pope John XIII late in 965, Pandulf sheltered him in Capua; the pope made Capua an archdiocese, and after ten months Pandulf sent him back with a Capuan escort. In 967 Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i">Otto I</a> granted him the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spoleto">Spoleto</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camerino">Camerino</a> and charged him with the war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>. On his brother's death in 968 he made his own eldest son Landulf co-prince at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a>. Left in charge of the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bari">Bari</a>, he was captured by the Byzantines at the Battle of Bovino in 969 and held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, while his wife Aloara and Archbishop Landulf I of Benevento governed. He was freed in the agreement that gave Theophanu in marriage to Otto II.</p><p>When his cousin Landulf of Conza deposed Gisulf of Salerno in 973, Pandulf restored Gisulf as his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, and on Gisulf's childless death he succeeded in Salerno as they had agreed. He then held all three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> principalities, with lands from Tuscany to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-taranto">Gulf of Taranto</a>. In 978 he confirmed the Sanctuary of Monte Sant'Angelo sul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gargano">Gargano</a> to the archbishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His sons fought over the inheritance. Landulf IV took Capua and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a> and Pandulf II Salerno, but when Otto II came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 981 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spoleto">Spoleto</a> went to Thrasimund IV, Benevento to Pandulf's nephew, and Manso I of Amalfi took Salerno. Another son, Atenulf, died at the Battle of Stilo on 13 July 982.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pandulf Ironhead” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Pandulf Ironhead</b> (died 981) briefly united Capua, Benevento and Spoleto under imperial patronage.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-capua">Principality of Capua</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Capua. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=971" aria-label="Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Pandulf Ironhead"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5788 3947h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5788 3947h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=971">What was here in 971 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
